\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 Slovak \highlight\uc2  c.bank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  to halt crown fall.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Peter Laca  \par \uc2 506 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 April 2005\par 10:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BRATISLAVA, April 29 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Friday to halt a weakening of the crown \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after outflow of hot money pushed it to a six-month low against the euro, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The bank's action was the first move to support its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in more than two years after it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly over the past year to curb what it saw as an excessively rapid crown appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  started buying crowns when it weakened to 40.0 per euro on Friday, levels last seen at the end of October.  \par \par \uc2 "The NBS has shown that it does not like the crown above 40.0... It can be expected that they will come again above that level," said Slovenska Sporitelna dealer Vladimir Gajdos.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which holds its monthly monetary policy meeting on Friday, was not immediately available for comment.  \par \par \uc2 The action pushed the crown as far as 39.400 per euro, but some traders said another move towards the 40.0 level was possible as foreign players might want to use the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to close positions accumulated during the crown's fast rise.  \par \par \uc2 The Slovak unit had strengthened quickly in the first weeks of the year, rising to a record high of 37.510 per euro in early March, which forced the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to use all its tools to halt the firming, including \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and an interest rate cut.  \par \par \uc2 But the crown has fallen more than 5 percent against the euro in the past month, as foreign investors have fled European emerging markets due to political uncertainty in the region and the prospect of rising interest rates in the United States.  \par \par \uc2 Market watchers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be seen as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  returning some euro liquidity into the thin market after it had bought more than 3 billion euros during its fight against crown firming between December and March.  \par \par \uc2 However, the estimated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  volume on Friday was small at up to 100 million euros.  \par \par \uc2 "This step should put a cap on SKK volatility, which has dramatically increased since mid-March," ING Bank Chief Economist Jan Toth wrote in a market note.  \par \par \uc2 ING does not expect the crown to firm quickly in the near future, partly because hundreds of millions of euros are expected to leave the market in dividend payments to foreign owners of Slovak companies in the next few weeks.  \par \par \uc2 However, analysts see the crown returning to the firming path later this year, helped by Slovakia's strong economic growth and expected foreign direct investment projects.  \par \par \uc2 "We might no longer see weak levels beyond 40 (per euro) in May, and a gradual strengthening towards 37 by year-end would be a logical move, taking into account the strong fundamental story," Toth said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0800 GMT the crown was quoted at 39.475/495 to the euro, compared with Thursday's close of 39.850/880.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050429e14t005ke\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
