\par\par \b \uc2 Slovakia - Factors To Watch on March 10.  \b0\par\par\uc2 408 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 08:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BRATISLAVA, March 10 (Reuters) - Here are news stories, press reports and events to watch which may affect Slovak financial markets on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 STATS OFFICE TO RELEASE Q4 AND FULL-YEAR 2004 GDP DATA  \par \par \uc2 The Statistics Office will release fourth quarter and full-year 2004 gross domestic product (GDP) data and will hold a news conference on the published figures.  \par \par \uc2 EU COMMISSIONER ALMUNIA VISITS SLOVAKIA  \par \par \uc2 European Commissioner for Economic and Monetary Affairs Joaquin Almunia will meet Slovak \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Ivan Sramko.  \par \par \uc2 FINMIN TO HOLD NEWS CONFERENCE  \par \par \uc2 The Slovak finance ministry will hold a news conference on results of public finances for 2004.  \par \par \uc2 ECON MINISTER TO ANNOUNCE NEW FDI PROJECT  \par \par \uc2 The economy ministry will hold a news conference to announce a new foreign direct investment project in eastern Slovakia.  \par \par \uc2 SLOVAK CBANKER SAYS INFLATION MAY ALLOW LOWER RATES  \par \par \uc2 The Slovak \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may cut interest rates all the way to the euro zone level if the inflation continues to develop better than expectated, the bank's Vice-governor Martin Barto said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SLOVAK\b0 \highlight\uc2  CBANKER UNHAPPY WITH \b \highlight22\uc2 FX INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  IMPACT  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBS) will continue its battle to stem a rising crown, but is unhappy with the diminishing impact of its frequent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Ivan Sramko said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SLOVAK\b0 \highlight\uc2  PM SAYS 2004 GDP ROSE 5.5 PCT - PAPER  \par \par \uc2 Slovak gross domestic product grew by an annual 5.5 percent in 2004, accelerating from 4.5 percent growth in the previous year, Prime Minister Mikulas Dzurinda said.  \par \par \uc2 SLOVAK JAN RETAIL SALES +7.7 PCT YR/YR  \par \par \uc2 Slovak retail sales rose 7.7 percent, year-on-year, in January, accelerating from 3.0 percent annual growth in December.  \par \par \uc2 For an economic indicator diary for the euro zone, the United States and other Group of Seven countries, see . For a diary of forthcoming Slovak events, double click  \par \par \uc2 , and a calendar of east European economic indicators, see.  \par \par \uc2 News editor of the day: Peter Laca on +421 2 5341 8402; fax: +421 2 5341 8403  \par \par \uc2 E-mail: bratislava.newsroom@reuters.com  \par \par \uc2 Reuters Messaging: peter.laca.reuters.com@reuters.net   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For real-time index quotes, double click in brackets: Warsaw WIG20\par Budapest BUX        Prague PX50         Other related news:\par  \par   Slovak equities              E.Europe equities\par   Slovak money                 Czech debt\par   Slovak Indicators            Emerging \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par   Eastern European             All emerging markets\par   Hot stocks                   Stock markets\par   Market debt news             \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news\par   TOP NEWS -- Emerging markets\par   TOP NEWS -- Convergence watch.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050310e13a006a9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
