\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Slovak \highlight\uc2  cbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  again to rein in crown.   \b0\par\par\uc2 358 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 2005\par 14:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BRATISLAVA, Feb 17 (Reuters) - The Slovak \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold crowns for euros directly on the market on Thursday in the latest stage of its long fight against fast \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation.   \par \par \uc2 The direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the third such action in the past two weeks which began when the crown rose close to all-time highs of 38.000 per euro, dealers said.   \par \par \uc2 "The reason for the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ongoing dissatisfaction with the exchange rate firming," \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spokesman Igor Barat told Reuters.   \par \par \uc2 The National Bank of Slovakia (NBS) has been trying to prevent what it calls unjustified crown appreciation for months, saying its pace is harmful for the economy and could endanger fulfillment of the euro adoption criteria.   \par \par \uc2 He added the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ready to use all its tools to halt unfavourable exchange rate developments.   \par \par \uc2 The Slovak unit shed roughly 0.3 percent to the euro following the NBS action, retreating as far as 38.135 per euro. It traded at 38.098/148 per euro at 1250 GMT.   \par \par \uc2 The monetary body has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the market twice in the last week to prevent the crown from firming beyond the psychological mark of 38.0 per euro. It has also used liquidity management \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make the unit less attractive for speculators.   \par \par \uc2 The crown has firmed 1.8 percent against the euro this year, boosted by Slovakia's improving economic performance, expectations of strong investment inflows from abroad and recent rating upgrades.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said the NBS move would weaken the unit only temporarily, adding they still expected it to advance to stronger levels in the longer-run.   \par \par \uc2 "The trend is still in favour of the crown firming. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's actions seem to me to be a fight with windmills," one dealer said.   \par \par \uc2 However, market watchers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to keep defending the key 38.0 per euro level in the near future, adding that an interest rate cut of at least 50 basis points was in the cards this month.   \par \par \uc2 Dealers estimated the NBS bought around 200 million euros in Thursday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050217e12h0073x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
