\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Slovak cbank keeps using repo to slow crown rise.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Peter Laca   \par \uc2 457 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2005\par 13:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BRATISLAVA, Feb 8 (Reuters) - The Slovak \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rejected all bids in a closely-watched repo tender on Tuesday for the second time in as many weeks, leaving a large liquidity surplus in the market in order to halt firming of the crown. The National Bank of Slovakia (NBS) has been trying to slow crown strengthening for more than a year, combining direct market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rate cuts and liquidity tools.   \par \par \uc2 It has repeatedly said that apart from protecting the economy from too sharp a rise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is also designed to ensure smooth fulfilment of inflation and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability criteria for adopting the euro.   \par \par \uc2 The crown has gained 1.9 percent against the euro so far this year, with the NBS saying the jump was caused mainly by inflows of hot money and expectations of future crown gains.   \par \par \uc2 "A large part of the short-term capital is being placed in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (liquidity-draining) repo tenders," NBS' policy-making Bank Board said in a statement.   \par \par \uc2 "The Bank Board considers the current crown appreciation as unjustified... and it remains determined to use available tools in case of further unjustified exchange rate appreciation."   \par \par \uc2 Bids in the two-week repo tender, which is the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's main monetary policy instrument, totalled 253.013 billion crowns ($8.5 billion) on Tuesday, more than twice as much as 116.107 billion crowns in last week's tender.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said the repo strategy could discourage speculators that were playing on higher short-term interest rates.   \par \par \uc2 But the majority of dealers predicted only a limited impact on the crown. They said most foreign players were buying the unit in expectations of future firming, fuelled by Slovakia's strong GDP growth and foreign direct investment projects.   \par \par \uc2 "We have seen a lot of London-based banks selling euros. They want to push the crown beyond 38.0 (per euro) expecting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to come in," said Tatra Banka dealer Roman Farkas. "It seems to me that they want to push the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut interest rates, where they have much bigger profit than on the spot."   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold crowns on the market on Monday when the crown threatened to firm through the psychological level of 38.0 to the euro. It was the second direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in less than a week and the third such move this year.   \par \par \uc2 The repo tender failed to weaken the crown, which traded at 38.035/050 to the euro as of 1215 GMT, slightly stronger from post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  opening level of 38.065/085. ($1=29.64 \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak\b0 \highlight\uc2  crowns) (Additional reporting by Martin Dokoupil).    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050208e12800b1a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
