\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Slovak \highlight\uc2  cbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  as crown nears record highs.   \b0\par\par\uc2 312 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Februar 2005\par 10:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BRATISLAVA, Feb 7 (Reuters) - Slovakia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to check the crown as it approached all-time highs on Monday, the latest move in a long battle to curb the rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Traders said the National Bank of Slovakia (NBS) stepped into the market when the crown traded at 38.020 against the euro, near record highs of 38.000.   \par \par \uc2 "We consider the pace of crown appreciation to be too fast and harmful for the economy," NBS spokesman Igor Barat said.   \par \par \uc2 Barat gave no details about the crown sales, which were the second direct action on the market in less than a week and the third \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year.   \par \par \uc2 He declined to comment whether the NBS did not want to see the crown firming through 38.000 per euro, but dealers said they expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep protecting the psychological level in the near future.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushed the crown as low as 38.140 per euro, but it quickly rebounded to 38.050/100.   \par \par \uc2 The crown has repeatedly hit new record highs in the past few weeks and it is up 1.8 percent versus the euro since the beginning of the year.   \par \par \uc2 The NBS has said it was slowing crown appreciation to avoid hurting the economy as well as to smooth the path to meeting inflation and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability criteria for adopting the euro.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said the NBS was selling crowns in several rounds on Monday. But they added there was strong interest in buying the Slovak unit at around 38.100 per euro, which prevented the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from having a more significant impact.   \par \par \uc2 "I don't see a longer-term impact... We will be under 38.000, it's just a matter of time," one dealer from a foreign bank said.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050207e12700afa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
