\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Zloty tracks bonds lower, Slovak crown edges up.  \b0\par\par\uc2 653 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2005\par 12:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 13 (Reuters) - The Polish zloty eased in line with a weaker tone on the domestic bond market on Thursday, while the Slovak crown got only a minor boost from a credit rating upgrade the previous session.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said jitters crept into the Polish fixed income market after reported comments from central banker Halina Wasilewska-Trenkner that interest rate cuts in 2005 looked unlikely.  \par \par \uc2 "Local players had got over long at the short-end of the interest rate curve, expecting a cut. These comments triggered a sell off in local debt, which is feeding into zloty," said one London trader.  \par \par \uc2 The zloty spiked down to lows around 4.0915 per euro , before recovering to trade at 4.0818 by 1103 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 "Euro/dollar is up and down, there's no clear trend there, so people are not putting much money to work in the zloty. When they do they're getting in and out quickly, which creates volatility," the trader said.  \par \par \uc2 He added the sharp moves seen on Thursday were not yet indicative of a reversal in the zloty's bullish trend: "Unless we cross 4.20, the move shouldn't be that much worry."  \par \par \uc2 The Turkish lira capitalised on a broadly weaker dollar tone following Wednesday's news of a record United States trade deficit in November, firming over one percent against the dollar in interbank trade.  \par \par \uc2 Investors remain bullish on Turkish markets following last month's decision by European Union leaders to grant Turkey a date to begin accession talks.  \par \par \uc2 The lira traded at 1.3550 to the dollar in interbank trade , from 1.3760 at Wednesday's close.  \par \par \uc2 SLOVAK CROWN EDGES UP  \par \par \uc2 The Slovak crown nudged higher against the euro on Thursday to trade around 38.6 , with more good news on the country's investment outlook coming from a Moody's ratings upgrade the previous session.  \par \par \uc2 Moody's lifted Slovakia's long-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt ceilings to "A2" from "A3", citing the government's solid progress on structural reforms and an improving medium-to-long-term fiscal outlook.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the upgrade was a mild positive, with the crown's upward path seen entrenched by strong foreign investment inflows, even with the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  still hanging over the market.  \par \par \uc2 "It's adding to the positive sentiment but I don't think this is the determining factor," said Jeff Gable, senior strategist at Barclays Capital.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday after the crown surged to record highs against the euro at 38.50.  \par \par \uc2 "In trying to take a view on Slovakia, what's most important presently is just how serious the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is at leaning against appreciation pressure and to gauge what pace of appreciation they consider acceptable," said Gable.  \par \par \uc2 "We think the direction of the Slovak crown on a weekly basis is pretty clean - it's going to go stronger," Gable said. The Czech crown eased slightly after Czech current account data showed a deficit of 6.05 billion crowns ($262.6 million) in November, nearly the double the consensus market forecast of 3.2 billion crowns, though down from 12.94 billion in October.  \par \par \uc2 Adding to the softer tone was \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board member Jan Frait's comment on Wednesday that there was little room for further crown appreciation against the euro and dollar based on economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 Frait also said in a Reuters interview that interest rates may stay low for some time to come to shield the EU newcomer's very open economy from any possible fallout from weaker growth abroad. Key rates are at 2.5 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "With interest rates being so slow, I think the case for significant crown appreciation from where we are now is a difficult one to make," Gable said.  \par \par \uc2 The Czech crown traded at 30.36 against the euro .   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050113e11d009t2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
