\par\par \b \uc2 Slovakia - Factors To Watch on Jan 13.  \b0\par\par\uc2 573 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2005\par 08:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BRATISLAVA, Jan 13 (Reuters) - Here are news stories, press reports and events to watch which may affect Slovak financial markets on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 ECON MIN TO ANNOUNCE NEW FDI PROJECT  \par \par \uc2 Economy Minister Pavol Rusko is scheduled to announce new foreign investment project in southern Slovakia.  \par \par \uc2 C.BANK SAYS \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTIONS\b0 \highlight\uc2  A SUCCESS, READY TO ACT AGAIN  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Ivan Sramko said on Wednesday recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the rise of the crown against the euro was a success. He said the monetary body would allow certain appreciation of the real exchange rate, but if the nominal exchange rate rises too quickly, it would use all tools to slow the rise.  \par \par \uc2 MOODY'S RAISES SLOVAKIA RATINGS  \par \par \uc2 Moody's Investors Service raised its ratings for Slovakia on Wednesday, saying the new EU nation may adopt the euro before its bigger central European neighbours which have drawn criticism for foot-dragging on reform.  \par \par \uc2 Moody's lifted its long-term foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt ceilings to A2 from A3 along with short term-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  country ceilings to P-1 from P-2. It left the outlook for ratings at positive.  \par \par \uc2 NOV RETAIL SALES GROWTH PICKS UP  \par \par \uc2 Slovak retail sales rose by a real 4.7 percent, year-on-year, in November, following a 3.1 percent growth in October, the Slovak Statistics Office said on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 NOV INDUSTRIAL WAGES GROWTH ACCELERATES  \par \par \uc2 Slovak industrial wages rose by a real 4.9 percent, year-on-year, in November, from a 0.2 percent growth in the previous month, data from the statistics office showed on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 GOVT MAY BOOST DEBT ISSUANCE TO COVER ARBITRATION LOSS  \par \par \uc2 Slovakia is unlikely to issue euro bonds this year and will instead boost debt issuance on the local market if it decides to honor an international legal claim, the state's debt agency said on Wednesday. An arbitration court ordered the country last month to pay 24.7 billion Slovak crowns to iron a lentghy loan dispute with a Czech unit of Belgium's KBC bank (KBKBt.BR).  \par \par \uc2 EMMIS AGREES TO BUY SLOVAK RADIO EXPRES FOR $14 MLN  \par \par \uc2 The U.S.-based media group Emmis Communications (EMMS.O) agreed to purchase the stock of the Slovak firm D.Expres, which operates commercial radio station Radio Expres for $14 million. The deal is expected to be completed in the first quarter of this year if it is cleared by the Slovak media regulator.  \par \par \uc2 FIN MIN TO BEGIN PROCEEDINGS AGAINST SLOVNAFT  \par \par \uc2 The finance ministry will begin on Friday proceedings to fine the Slovak oil refiner Slovnaft (SNFT.BV), a unit of Hungary's MOL (MOLB.BU), 1.35 billion crowns ($45.7 million) for illegal inflating of costs and profits.  \par \par \uc2 Sme page 5  \par \par \uc2 United States and other Group of Seven countries, see .  \par \par \uc2 For a diary of forthcoming Slovak events, double click  \par \par \uc2 , and a calendar of east European economic indicators, see.  \par \par \uc2 News editor of the day: Martin Dokoupil on +421 2 5341 8402; fax: +421 2 5341 8403  \par \par \uc2 E-mail: bratislava.newsroom@reuters.com  \par \par \uc2 Reuters Messaging: martin.dokoupil.reuters.com@reuters.net   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For real-time index quotes, double click in brackets: Warsaw WIG20\par Budapest BUX        Prague PX50         Other related news:\par  \par   Slovak equities              E.Europe equities\par   Slovak money          