\par\par \b \uc2 Slovakia - Factors To Watch on Jan 12.  \b0\par\par\uc2 578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2005\par 08:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  BRATISLAVA, Jan 12 (Reuters) - Here are news stories, press reports and events to watch which may affect Slovak financial markets on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2  STATS OFFICE TO RELEASE NOV RETAIL SALES DATA   \par \par \uc2  The statistics office will release November retail sales data. The retail sales growth decelerated to 3.1 percent, year-on-year, in October, from 8.9 percent in the previous month.   \par \par \uc2  GOVT TO HOLD REGULAR MEETING   \par \par \uc2  The government will hold its regular weekly meeting. Ministers are expected to debate the recent arbitration loss to a Czech unit of KBC Bank, aid to tsunami victims in Asia and a progress report on completion of the Mochovce nuclear power plant.   \par \par \uc2  C.BANK ACCEPTS ALL BIDS IN 2-WK REPO   \par \par \uc2  The Slovak \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  accepted all bids in its regular two-week repo tender on Tuesday, draining 143.2 billion crowns ($4.86 billion) of surplus liquidity from the market.   \par \par \uc2  FIN MIN SAYS CROWN FIRMING TOO STRONG   \par \par \uc2  The Slovak crown's firming is too strong and could hurt the new EU member's economy, Finance Minister Ivan Miklos said. He added that one of the biggest challenges for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be to prevent the crown from firming excessively to the euro in the future.   \par \par \uc2  SLOVAK PM SAYS GOVT TO KEEP UP PACE OF REFORMS   \par \par \uc2  The fractious Slovak government will not slow the pace of its final sweeping reforms to bring the country toward euro adoption, Prime Minister Mikulas Dzurinda said.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SLOVAK INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  DIPS MAKE GOOD CROWN BUY POINTS   \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may pull out all the stops to stem a firming crown, but strong investment flows and economic growth in the new EU member make any dips on a good time to jump into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said.   \par \par \uc2  GOVT SEEKS TO DELAY NUCLEAR PLANT CLOSURE   \par \par \uc2  Slovakia is seeking to persuade the EU to delay the closure for safety reasons of two Soviet-designed nuclear reactor blocks at the Jaslovske Bohunice plant, a senior government official said on Tuesday. Pal Csaky, deputy prime minister for European integration, said Slovakia had signed a memorandum with the EU to extend financing needed for Bohunice decommissioning until 2011.   \par \par \uc2  NOV TRADE GAP WIDENS, ABOVE FCASTS   \par \par \uc2  Slovakia's foreign trade balance showed a deficit of 3.953 billion crowns ($134.5 million) in November, up from a revised gap of 3.377 billion in the previous month.   \par \par \uc2  NOV INDUSTRIAL OUTPUT GROWTH PICKS UP   \par \par \uc2  Slovak November industrial output grew 2.5 percent, year-on-year, up from a 0.2 percent rise in the previous month, data from the Slovak Statistics Office showed on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2  NOV CONSTRUCTION OUTPUT GROWTH SLOWS   \par \par \uc2  The Slovak construction output growth slowed to 9.8 percent, year-on-year, in November, from 13.7 percent in the previous month.   \par \par \uc2  United States and other Group of Seven countries, see .   \par \par \uc2  For a diary of forthcoming Slovak events, double click   \par \par \uc2  , and a calendar of east European economic indicators, see.   \par \par \uc2  News editor of the day: Martin Dokoupil on +421 2 5341 8402; fax: +421 2 5341 8403   \par \par \uc2  E-mail: bratislava.newsroom@reuters.com   \par \par \uc2  Reuters Messaging: martin.dokoupil.reuters.com@reuters.net   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For real-time index quotes, double click in brackets: Warsaw WIG20\par Budapest