\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS- \highlight22\uc2 Slovak intervention \highlight\uc2  dips make good crown buy points.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jan Lopatka  \par \uc2 697 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Januar 2005\par 16:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 PRAGUE, Jan 11 (Reuters) - The Slovak \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may pull out all the stops to stem a firming crown, but strong investment flows and economic growth in the new EU member make any dips on a good time to jump into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought 100-250 million euros from the market on Monday, according to traders' estimates, in the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  against what it calls the excessive pace of firming, mostly made on foreign direct investment inflows.  \par \par \uc2 The action was the first under new chief Ivan Sramko, who took over this month from outgoing Marian Jusko, and suggested the bank was mostly sticking to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  course seen throughout last year although analysts said the stance against the crown may become a little softer.  \par \par \uc2 A strong economy, growing at 5.3 percent in the third quarter, and the FDI makes the crown a safe bet over the longer term, and any dip versus the euro creates a good entry level for positions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 Morgan Stanley emerging markets analyst Oliver Weeks said the country's inflation targets for 2006-2007 of 2.5 and 2.0 percent were the toughest in the region and implied a tight monetary stance, which could mean a more relaxed approach to the crown by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "While the new Bank Board and Governor will not want to encourage \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation by a clear shift in policy we see room for a gradually increasing tolerance of steady crown appreciation," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came when the crown hit an all-time high of 38.500 to the euro , prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  into action, knocking it back to 38.640 on Tuesday, still more than six percent up since the start of 2004.  \par \par \uc2 A little softer stance on further firming would give further support to long-crown trades.  \par \par \uc2 This view was also shared by Ceska Sporitelna analyst Miroslav Plojhar.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  they have been doing are a way how to jump into the crown," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "There already is some tolerance to crown firming... Maybe it is higher now that Jusko has gone, but this is not to mean they would just let the crown be."  \par \par \uc2 The Slovak drive to slash inflation is guided by their aim of joining the euro zone in 2009. The target this year is 3.0-4.0 percent, from 6.3 percent reported in November.  \par \par \uc2 Therefore, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to slash interest rates much further from the current four percent, a 200 basis points worth of cuts over the past year.  \par \par \uc2 SOME RESISTANCE NEEDED  \par \par \uc2 Slovakia became a darling of foreign investors due to labour market and tax reforms as well as government investment aid.  \par \par \uc2 Total net FDI in the first nine months last year reached 34.3 billion crowns and the financial account surplus was 56.6 billion, versus current account gap of 27.7 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Martin Blum, head of European emerging markets research at HVB in Vienna said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was essentially doing a good job given the narrow scope of options amid the balance of payments pressure and with interest rates already low.  \par \par \uc2 "You could envisage a scenario whereby if the national bank failed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market at all, you would really have euro/Slovak accelerating its movement south," he said, adding the banks still favoured long crown positions.  \par \par \uc2 "It would potentially push inflation down too fast and more importantly, it would risk future reversal which is not something the national bank would want to deal with either."  \par \par \uc2 Blum added the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely allow something like the same pace of appreciation as seen in 2004. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself has said real firming of 2-3 percent per year looked acceptable.  \par \par \uc2 "It is reasonable to use these \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a device to slow the appreciation of the crown which is what they were trying to do in 2004 as well, and I think they are doing basically the most sensible thing in quite a constrained situation," said Blum. - Additional reporting by Jan Korselt.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050111e11b00hra\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
