\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Lira edges up after rate cut, Slovak crown steady.  \b0\par\par\uc2 496 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Januar 2005\par 12:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 11 (Reuters) - The Turkish lira nudged higher after a surprise interest rate cut on Tuesday, while the \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak\b0 \highlight\uc2  crown was flat after Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken it.  \par \par \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut both its overnight borrowing rate and overnight lending rate by one percent to 17 percent and 21 percent respectively.  \par \par \uc2 The timing of the move took analysts by surprise, coming hard on the heels of a two percent cut on Dec. 20 following the European Union's decision to grant Turkey a date to begin entry talks, though some said there had been scope for further easing.  \par \par \uc2 "We are currently in a situation in Turkey where real rates are fairly steady. Inflation expectations are very close to end-year expectations. There was also a tightening impact coming from the strengthening of the lira. There were all the key ingredients to have a rate cut," said Sonal Desai, senior emerging markets economist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein in Milan.  \par \par \uc2 "It'll have a positive impact on Turkish markets, but the impact on the lira is close to zero," she added.  \par \par \uc2 The move triggered a half percentage point fall in benchmark Turkish bond yields and also boosted Istanbul's main share index, though the lira ticked only modestly higher.  \par \par \uc2 By 1040 GMT, the lira traded at 1.3815 to the dollar on the interbank market from 1.3850 at Monday's close.  \par \par \uc2 The prospect of further inflows into Turkish bonds, on expectations of more easing to come, is seen offsetting the potential negative effect of lower returns on long lira positions.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SLOVAK\b0 \highlight\uc2  CROWN STEADIES AFTER \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday after the crown surged to record highs against the euro around 38.5 , triggering a move to 38.74 initially, before the crown recovered some ground. The rate stabilised around 38.70 on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 A bigger than expected November trade gap was seen preventing a further recovery in the crown, with the deficit widening to 3.953 billion crowns ($134.5 million), above a market consensus forecast of 2.7 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Even so, most analysts continue to see the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fighting an unstoppable strengthening trend in the Slovak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , driven by strong foreign investment inflows.  \par \par \uc2 Carlos Teixeira, analyst at Goldman Sachs, said the Slovaks' latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was no surprise.  \par \par \uc2 "Their action may stem appreciation temporarily, as last happened in December, but we expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to resume its strengthening," Teixeira said in a research note. "This will help the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meet its inflation target and leaves the door open to further rate cuts."  \par \par \uc2 The crown traded at 38.69 per euro.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar peeled back more of last week's strong gains against the euro , lending a broadly firmer tone to high yield emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The South African rand was again the best performer, rising 0.82 percent against the dollar to trade around 5.94 .   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050111e11b00cch\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
