\par\par \b \uc2 Slovakia - Factors To Watch on Jan 11.  \b0\par\par\uc2 524 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Januar 2005\par 08:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  BRATISLAVA, Jan 11 (Reuters) - Here are news stories, press reports and events to watch which may affect Slovak financial markets on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2  C.BANK TO HOLD 2-WK REPO TENDER   \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hold its regular two-week repo tender, which is its main monetary policy tool. The bank used the weekly liquidity-draining auction in the past to dent firming pressure on the crown \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  STATS OFFICE TO RELEASE NOV TRADE DATA   \par \par \uc2  The statistics office will release November foreign trade data. Analysts surveyed by Reuters expected a trade deficit of 2.7 billion crowns in November, up from a 1.998 billion gap in the previous month.   \par \par \uc2  STATS OFFICE TO RELEASE NOV INDUSTRIAL OUTPUT   \par \par \uc2  The statistics office will release November industrial output data. Analysts surveyed by Reuters predicted industrial output growth to accelerate to 3.5 percent on an annual basis in November, from 0.2 percent in October.   \par \par \uc2  STATS OFFICE TO RELEASE NOV CONSTRUCTION OUTPUT   \par \par \uc2  The statistics office will release November construction output data. The indicator showed a rise of 13.7 percent on an annual basis in October.   \par \par \uc2  PM DZURINDA TO HOLD NEWS CONFERENCE   \par \par \uc2  Slovak Prime Minister Mikulas Dzurinda is scheduled to hold a news conference on government plans for 2005.   \par \par \uc2  RULING COALITION LEADERS MEET   \par \par \uc2  The ruling coalition leaders hold their regular meeting.   \par \par \uc2  C.BANK \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENES\b0 \highlight\uc2  AGAIN TO SLOW CROWN   \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBS) on Monday continued its crusade against the rapid rise of the crown, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the market to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after it hit record highs against the euro. The crown briefly touched to an all-time high of 38.500 per euro earlier on Monday.   \par \par \uc2  FIN MIN SEES 2004 FISCAL GAP BELOW TARGET   \par \par \uc2  The Slovak 2004 public sector deficit should end below the country's target of 3.9 percent of gross domestic product before a big one-off dent from an international legal claim.   \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Ivan Miklos also said the government was still studying the arbitration result, which ordered it to pay 24.7 billion crowns ($845 million) to the Czech unit of Belgium's KBC Bank, and would discuss next week whether to challenge it or not.   \par \par \uc2  ENEL AGREES SE SALE CONDITIONS   \par \par \uc2  Italy's Enel (ENEI.MI) has agreed all conditions of the sale of Slovak power utility Slovenske Elektrarne. Slovak Economy Minister Pavol Rusko said he expected Enel to complete the takeover in the second half of this year.   \par \par \uc2  United States and other Group of Seven countries, see .   \par \par \uc2  For a diary of forthcoming Slovak events, double click   \par \par \uc2  , and a calendar of east European economic indicators, see.   \par \par \uc2  News editor of the day: Martin Dokoupil on +421 2 5341 8402; fax: +421 2 5341 8403   \par \par \uc2  E-mail: bratislava.newsroom@reuters.com   \par \par \uc2  Reuters Messaging: martin.dokoupil.reuters.com@reuters.net   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For real-time index quotes, double click in brackets: Warsaw WIG20\par Budapest BUX        Prague PX50         Other related news:\par  \par   Slovak equities              E.Europe equities\par   Slovak money                 Czech debt\par   Slovak Indicators            Emerging \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlig