\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Rand and lira test highs -  \highlight22\uc2 Slovak \highlight\uc2  cbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2 .  \b0\par\par\uc2 687 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Dezember 2004\par 12:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Dec 23 (Reuters) - High-yield emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  firmed against a broadly weak dollar on Thursday, with the rand and lira both testing fresh highs while the \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the crown for a second day.  \par \par \uc2 The euro rose to within a quarter-cent of record highs against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in technically-driven trade as dealers wound down for the year-end holiday period.  \par \par \uc2 South Africa's rand coined a new six-year high of 5.6110 per dollar late on Wednesday and hovered close to that mark in light trade on Thursday. By 1100 GMT, it had eased a shade to trade at 5.6238 .  \par \par \uc2 "The rand will probably go marginally weaker from here, but I don't see a major sell off," said Gregory Kronsten, emerging markets analyst at WestLB.  \par \par \uc2 Kronsten added that South Africa's combination of high interest rates and a rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had proved a strong enticement to long-term investors, helping fuel rand gains of over 18 percent against the dollar in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 November producer price data did little to change a growing view among analysts that key interest rates may now have bottomed out at 7.5 percent.  \par \par \uc2 South African November producer prices rose at an annual rate of 2.5 percent, up from 1.9 percent the previous month, and followed a faster-than-expected 4.6 percent year-on-year rise in the November CPIX indicator, released on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 "Some people think that because the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't cut in December, they'll cut in February, but I don't expect any cooling in domestic demand by then," said Kronsten.  \par \par \uc2 "My own view is that easing has come to an end and that rates could go up a bit towards the end of next year."  \par \par \uc2 A broadly weaker dollar helped the Turkish lira firm to its highest level since May, continuing its strong run following last Friday's decision by European Union leaders to let the country start talks on Oct. 3 2005 to join to the bloc.  \par \par \uc2 The lira traded at 1,381,100 to the dollar on the interbank market from 1,390,000.  \par \par \uc2 SLOVAK C.BANK DENTS CROWN  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  followed up Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the crown by selling it again at weaker levels around 38.70 per euro .  \par \par \uc2 Its action moved the euro/crown rate up to 38.85 initially, though analysts are sceptical the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can do any more than slow the pace of crown gains such is the volume of foreign investment inflows into the country.  \par \par \uc2 The Polish zloty drifted down from late Wednesday's near-two-year high against the euro, hit after Finance Minister Miroslaw Gronicki appeared relaxed about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's charge higher this week.  \par \par \uc2 Gronicki said the move reflected year-end effects from low-volume trade, while analysts continue to see little on the near-term horizon to halt the Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bull run. "The bottom line is there's not a consensus in policy making circles that they want a weaker zloty," said Martin Blum, emerging markets analyst at HVB in Vienna.  \par \par \uc2 "The zloty has actually appreciated very little in real terms over the past five years and with the productivity gains seen in Poland it's still not overvalued. It's also helped bring down public debt-to-GDP levels and that helps explain Gronicki's comments."   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The zloty traded at 4.0851 to the euro  from 4.0793 late on Wednesday\par and at 3.0365 to the dollar        from 3.0453.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Blum said the zloty could target further gains to 3.90 per euro in the first quarter of 2005.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "As (the euro/zloty rate) moves further south we will have policymakers becoming more aggressive in talking about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "But the headwind won't prevent further downside. There's a lot of inflows into emerging Europe funds, the political backdrop in Poland is OK, yields are still OK and there's also a generally favourable environment for emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041223e0cn00avp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
