\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Slovak \highlight\uc2  crown weaker after c.bank  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 .  \b0\par\par\uc2 258 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Dezember 2004\par 17:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BRATISLAVA, Dec 22 (Reuters) - The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Slovak\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  crown weakened on\par Wednesday after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  directly on the\par market to slow the unit's appreciation, but dealers said they\par did not expect further major losses in the coming days.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  entered the market with crown sales when\par its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traded near all-time highs of around 38.550 per\par euro early in the session.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  briefly pulled the crown to around 38.900\par per euro, but weaker levels attracted new crown purchases by\par both foreign and domestic banks and the demand pushed the unit\par to a close of 38.720/750 to the euro.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it had \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to slow the crown's\par rise as it considered too fast appreciation as dangerous for the\par economy.\par     Wednesday's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rounds were the first such direct\par action since July when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bought foreign\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  worth a combined 766 million euros.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The Slovak unit hit an all-time high of 38.530 per euro on\par Monday, and dealers expected firming trend to resume early in\par 2005.\par     "The crown should stabilise at around 38.700 for the next\par few days, but we expect it to return to around 38.500 at the\par start of the new year, and then firm beyond that level," said\par Slovenska Sporitelna dealer Juraj Zabadal.\par     Thursday will be the last trading day before Christmas\par holidays in Slovakia, and dealers do not expect major trading\par activity next week before the end of the year.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041222e0cm00iip\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
