\par\par \b \uc2 Slovak crown eases, seen locked in narrow range.  \b0\par\par\uc2 381 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2004\par 17:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BRATISLAVA, July 22 (Reuters) - The Slovak crown closed a\par touch softer to the euro in quiet summer holiday trading on\par Thursday, and dealers said a fresh impulse would be needed to\par drive the unit out of its current narrow range.\par     The crown has been hovering in a tight corridor of\par 39.800-39.900 to the euro for the past few days as the market\par sought new reference levels following the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's heavy\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  early last week.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Slovak\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  unit firmed to 39.830 per euro early on Thursday\par after a London-based bank started selling euros, but later\par slipped to 39.870 on euro purchases by a local bank.\par     Dealers said the crown, which hit a record high of 39.675\par per euro earlier this month, should drift in its recent range in\par the coming days in the absence of market-moving news.\par     "The trading should be calm until next Friday, when the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  holds its monetary meeting. But there is nothing\par special expected," said ING Bank dealer Jan Rafaj.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     All analysts surveyed by Reuters earlier in July expected\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stay on hold this month after it cut rates\par by 50 basis points in June to dent the crown's firming\par potential.\par  \par     The bank has eased credit costs by 150 basis points this\par year to stop hot-money inflows from boosting the crown.\par     However, policy makers recently resorted to direct crown\par selling after the wave of rate cuts, off-market crown sales and\par repo tenders failed to weaken the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as hoped.\par     But dealers said the bank would have no reason to consider\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 further market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the crown, which is up 3.2  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 percent to the euro this year, remains close to its current  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 levels.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bank has been battling the rapid rise of the crown for  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 months, saying its pace was too quick despite improving economic  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 fundamentals.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - MARKET SNAPSHOT AT 1435 GMT-  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crown/euro at 39.850/900 vs 39.831/880 on Wednesday  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crown/dollar at 32.433/474 vs 32.556/597  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 5-yr yield March 2008 at 4.125/3.976 pct vs 4.160/090  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10-yr yld July 2013 at 5.152/049 pct vs 5.142/4.892  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040722e07m002hp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
