\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Zloty holds firm pre vote, forint awaits data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 606 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juli 2004\par 11:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, July 15 (Reuters) - The Polish zloty held firm close to 10-month highs against the euro on Thursday ahead of a key vote on pension reforms in parliament, while the Hungarian forint traded quietly ahead of key budget data.  \par \par \uc2 The zloty extended this week's strong gains on Wednesday after news of a jump in June consumer price inflation stoked rate hike expectations. The annual inflation rate hit 4.4 percent, above market forecasts and up from 3.4 percent in May. A calmer tone set in on Thursday with dealers looking ahead to a vote in parliament on Friday on pension reforms - a key pillar of the government's efforts to curb spending.  \par \par \uc2 "The pension reform vote will only be a factor if it fails, we think it's largely been priced in," said Tania Kotsos, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Royal Bank of Canada.  \par \par \uc2 "The zloty is behaving as a carry trade, especially in the summer months with people happy just to hold their positions to pick up the yield."  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the June CPI number increased the chances of a further rate hike in July to follow June's 50 basis point increase, though Monetary Policy Council member Halina Wasilewska-Trenkner said she expected price pressures to begin to ease in August.  \par \par \uc2 Kotsos said that on a technical basis, the zloty needed a decisive break under 4.4250 to open up levels of 4.40 and beyond.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By 0915 GMT, the zloty traded at 4.43 to the euro  from 4.4395 and at\par 3.5870 to the dollar        from 3.5880.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FORINT EYES BUDGET DATA  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Hungarian forint hovered around the 250 per euro level, with the market seen subdued ahead of final June and preliminary July budget data due at 1100 GMT.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts said the sacking of two finance ministry officials on Wednesday was perhaps an indicator of bad news to come, with the market already sceptical about the government's chances of hitting a 4.6 percent-of-GDP deficit target for 2004.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It is suspicious and could make things worse for the budget, but it's uncertain at this stage," said Caio Natividade, emerging markets \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst at Deutsche Bank.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There has also been talk that the departure of Elemer Tertak, administrative state secretary with responsibility for the budget, and his deputy Akos Cseres, was simply a case of a personality clash with Finance Minister Tibor Draskovics and would have no bearing on Thursday's numbers.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Natividade said that while there was downside risk in the forint, he still favoured this week's uptrend to remain intact: "As far as momentum is concerned, the market is going to 247.50."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Speculation is also mounting that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may take advantage of the forint's move to two-month highs against the euro this week to trim interest rates at its next policy meeting on Monday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key rates are at a lofty 11.5 percent having been jacked up to deter outflows from the bond market and the forint.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The forint traded was little changed at 249.55 to the euro .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Slovak crown held to narrow ranges around the 39.90 area against the euro with the market still nervous about the potential for further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Slovak\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  May trade gap came in at a bigger-than-expected 2.1 billion crowns, but with some analysts expecting foreign direct investment inflows to outpace the current account deficit by a ratio of 3:1 this year, the crown uptrend is expected to remain intact.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040715e07f000xc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
